WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.560
Defenders should think in terms of data flows, not only servers.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:20.660
Sensitive data may move over internet traffic, VPN tunnels, API calls, email, file transfers, cloud storage synchronization, database replication, partner integrations, service-to-service communication, mobile application traffic, and IoT or embedded device links.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:30.560
A server may be well managed, but the data path can still include networks, proxies, logs, archives, third-party services, and client environments that shape exposure.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:39.420
TLS-protected web traffic is a common place to start because websites, APIs, gateways, and service meshes often carry valuable information.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:47.720
VPN traffic is also relevant because it may include administrative access, internal applications, or remote workforce activity.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:54.340
Email and file transfer systems can move legal records, contracts, health data, credentials, and business plans.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:00.560
Backup and replication traffic can carry large volumes of sensitive information over predictable paths.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.740
Logs and archives can matter as much as live traffic.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:13.400
A network appliance, proxy, packet capture platform, troubleshooting workflow, security tool, or cloud service may preserve metadata or payloads longer than expected.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.660
Third-party systems can also change the exposure model.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:29.680
If sensitive information crosses a partner integration, managed service, CDN, cloud storage platform, or SaaS product, the organization's risk depends partly on external cryptographic and retention practices.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:38.060
A practical assessment maps where sensitive data is created, transmitted, transformed, stored, replicated, and retained.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:49.400
That map should include encryption boundaries, termination points, certificate use, VPN endpoints, API gateways, service identities, backup flows, and vendor-managed paths.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.660
The goal is not to assume every path is equally urgent.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:58.000
The goal is to find the paths where long-lived sensitive data could be collected today and exposed later.
